meta {
  name: 03 - Direct RLS: owner A reading tenant B resources via PostgREST
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{supabaseUrl}}/rest/v1/resources?tenant_id=eq.{{tenantBId}}&select=*
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  apikey: {{supabaseAnonKey}}
  Authorization: Bearer {{ownerAToken}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("PostgREST returns 200, not 401/403", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("row-level security silently filters out tenant B's resources", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.be.an("array").that.is.empty;
  });
}

docs {
  This request bypasses the NestJS backend entirely — it hits Supabase's
  PostgREST REST API directly with the tenant A owner's real JWT, using
  the anon key (never the service role key). This is the only way to
  prove `tenant_isolation_resources` (0001_init.sql) does the isolating,
  independent of TenantGuard/RolesGuard.

  The earlier Bruno tests (00-02) only prove the NestJS guards work —
  they use the backend's service role connection, which bypasses RLS
  entirely. A 401/403 from those tests says nothing about whether
  Postgres itself would enforce the same boundary if some other client
  (this request, or any future one) ever queried the database directly
  with a real user JWT instead of going through the backend.

  Expected result: HTTP 200 with an EMPTY array — not a 401 or 403.
  RLS does not reject the request; it silently removes rows the caller
  isn't allowed to see. A non-empty result here would mean the
  `tenant_isolation_resources` policy is not doing its job, regardless
  of what the application layer enforces on top of it. Companion test:
  04-direct-rls-own-tenant-visible (same request, tenant A's own id) —
  needed to prove this endpoint isn't just broken/denying everything.
}
